"The prognosis is influenced by several factors, including a preoperative PSA of 4.5 and an ISUP grade group 2 pathology from the prostatectomy specimen. Positive findings include negative surgical margins and no seminal vesicle invasion. Countervailing points are that no lymph nodes were removed, meaning nodal status is unknown, which widens uncertainty. The biopsy-to-prostatectomy comparison shows concordance in Gleason pattern (3+4/ISUP 2). Prognostic revision would be significantly aided by postoperative PSA levels and their subsequent trajectory. Currently, the lack of documented follow-up or postoperative PSA prevents a more refined assessment. The fixed estimated horizon is 84.76 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 50.86–118.66 months; its coverage has not been validated."
